Sell tickets and collect event registrations with @@NAME@@ forms. Add a Gutenberg block or shortcode to your WordPress site.

== Description ==

Sell event tickets and collect registrations directly on your WordPress website with an embedded @@NAME@@ form. Add the Gutenberg block to a page or post, or use a shortcode with your saved public form URL.

Use the same workflow for balls, concerts, theatre performances, festivals, workshops and conferences. Create the event and its ticket or registration form in @@NAME@@, then display that form on your own website.

The free plugin connects WordPress to the @@MARKET@@ @@NAME@@ service. An organizer account and a saved, published form are required. No WooCommerce or service API keys are needed. The external service has its own organizer pricing and terms; see @@OFFER_URL@@ for its current offer. WordPress does not process service fees.

= Features =

* Ticket sales and event registration forms on WordPress pages and posts.
* A native Gutenberg block and a shortcode using the same saved form URL.
* Responsive embeds with automatic height or a fixed height you choose.
* Multiple forms on one page and a standalone link to open each form separately.
* A descriptive form title for screen readers.
* Website appearance controlled centrally in the saved form settings.
* A Czech interface for plugin setup and the block editor.
* Optional measurement integration for websites with their own GTM and consent banner.

= Get started in three steps =

1. Create your event in @@NAME@@ and save a public ticket or registration form.
2. Copy its public URL, add the @@NAME@@ block to WordPress and paste the URL. A shortcode is available if you do not use the block editor.
3. Preview the form on desktop and mobile, then publish your page.

Local setup help is available under Settings > @@NAME@@ and through the Get started link in the plugin list. You do not need to paste administration links or sign in to the service inside the WordPress editor.

= Tickets, registrations and seating plans =

The embedded form follows the event configuration in @@NAME@@, including ticket types, available capacity and any configured seating plan. Event management, order processing, payments, ticket delivery and admission remain in the external service.

The plugin does not implement a payment gateway. Payment methods depend on the organizer's configuration and the service. Verify the complete purchase flow for your event and browser, including any card gateway or bank-app handoff, before opening sales.

= Gutenberg block and shortcode =

Paste the exact saved public form URL on @@ORIGIN@@. Query parameters, fragments, personal order links and administration URLs are not accepted.

Example:

    [@@SHORTCODE@@ url="@@ORIGIN@@/form/saved-slug" title="Tickets for our event" measurement="host"]

Fixed height:

    [@@SHORTCODE@@ url="@@ORIGIN@@/form/saved-slug" mode="fixed" height="800" measurement="host"]

= Appearance and mobile layout =

Automatic height follows the form content. Fixed and fallback height can be set between 240 and 4000 pixels. Both modes use the service helper; a standalone form link remains available if the helper is blocked.

To match your website, open the form appearance settings in @@NAME@@, choose Match website appearance and save the form. The setting applies to every embed on subsequent page loads without replacing the block or shortcode. WordPress cannot override it. The seating plan keeps its own appearance.

= Optional measurement =

New blocks and the shortcode examples use Website dataLayer, recommended for websites with their own GTM and consent banner. Existing blocks and shortcodes without a measurement setting retain the Festapp Unit runtime. Use measurement="unit" to select Unit mode; missing or invalid values also retain Unit mode.

The measurement setting selects a destination; it does not enable tracking or grant consent. Both modes require an approved website origin and enabled measurement in Festapp. Unit mode reads website Consent Mode only after the organizer enables Use consent from my website banner in Festapp and confirms that the banner covers the embedded form.

= Documentation and support =

@@DOCUMENTATION@@

= External service and privacy =

On a published page with a configured form, the visitor's browser connects to @@ORIGIN@@ to load /form/saved-slug?embed=1. Both height modes also load @@ORIGIN@@/embed.js once per page. This connection shares the visitor's IP address, browser information and any referrer allowed by the site's policy. The visitor enters purchase data directly into the service iframe. The helper exchanges resize/viewport/scroll, optional bounded presentation/font settings and displayed ticket counts/prices with the form. In Unit measurement mode, customer contacts and transaction identifiers remain outside the host page. Explicitly selecting Website dataLayer forwards approved measurement events, including conversion email, phone and transaction identifiers, to page scripts. Payment instructions, authentication secrets and order submissions are never forwarded.

The service may use its configured backend, assets, storage and payment providers. Review its privacy information and the needs of your own website before publishing; this plugin does not claim that the service is cookie-free. The plugin installs no tracking tags; in Website dataLayer mode the website owns its tags and consent handling. Unit mode uses the configured Festapp runtime and requires the organizer's explicit consent-bridge setting before reading website Consent Mode. The external service has its own privacy practices, described in its privacy policy. Optional website appearance loads the website font URLs inside the service iframe. Activation, local help and editing do not fetch the remote form. WordPress stores the public URL and presentation attributes, not customer orders, organizer passwords or service API keys. No server-side HTTP request is made by the plugin.

